nt so we also have  a snip report  and the snippet port make this a little  bit bigger for myself here  so the snipper port is  interactive with the alignment views  and so if i click on any one of these  rows which is a separate variant it will  bring me to the sequence alignment view  and i can go between different  chromosomes and we can see i'll just  draw some arrows here  but there is  filters at the top  and we have information at the bottom  and each column contains a different  piece of information about the variant  and each row is a separate variant  okay and then the filters at the top are  the controls that the user has to now  filter for some of the snips and so we  can see you know the number of snips  that are present  in  in the number of columns that are  present  and let me actually undo that  so we can see there's 37 snip columns  rejected and you know how many have been  filtered out and so we can keep track of  the number of snips that we have you  know in this view and the different  filters at the top can include things  like  depth of coverage  and we can have a minimum and max snip  percentage  as well we have  over here show  coding change snaps so i can if i have  an annotated reference i can  differentiate between snips that cause a  coding change snips that do not  um show all snips so these are snips  that are both in db snip and snips that  are potentially novel  and so by applying different filters  probability p not ref  i can really filter down and focus on  those variants of interest and what i  mentioned earlier is that what's nice  about  seekman software is that i can start  very uh with allowing almost all the  variations or all the sequencing errors  into the project and get a good feel for  you know how much sequence noise do i  have and where do i really start losing  my signal you know how much can i  actually filter without risking you know  losing some signal so  it's a really nice interface to  experiment with that now 