res so  again this is some of the nice database  linkage uh that we're using  right now  um but you can see with with with  seekman it allows us to look at all the  assemblies um  but when you start doing larger projects  or if i've got  several thousand snips in each one of my  samples um it's very difficult to  manually go through and you know and  look at a thousand different locations  you know it's most people don't have  time or patients to do something at that  scale  so you know back when we were developing  our human genome assembler  we quickly realized there was a need to  be able to assess the assembly and uh to  look at the snips that were found in one  version of the assembler and then make  some improvements and see you know did  it get better is it the same  and and again look at those variations  look at curated  hat map data sets you know  exome and genome level you know to make  sure that we're finding all the snip so  we you know you want to develop  something that's that's highly accurate  as well  and so  we developed some tools in array star in  house array star has a lot of capability  for importing data files that are in uh  you know tab delimited format for  instance right so we can we can import  projects into a race star and it can be  you know i say 2 to 200 we haven't  tested above 200 but there's no reason  to believe it can't go above 200 what  our largest data sets are right now  and you can see i imported my three iron  torrent ample seat cancer panels  i could bring in and set this experiment  up more sophisticated if i want and so  there's a little button up here  in a race star and it says that allows  me to import attributes you know for  these experiments so so i can go to a  text delimited file and and  point to that and it'll bring up this  wizard to say well what you know which  columns are interested and this looks  like a control that i've used for my  control set here some different ethnic  groups of human genomes  and so so i can bring in all different  experiment attributes by defining um  my data files that way  array star then allows me to you know  filter across multiple samples now  at much larger scales  and so it has its own filtering dialogue  and what's very different about a race  star though is up here in the corner  i can filter  at two different levels  i can filter at a gene level or it can  filter at a snip level  so array star has  keeps track of things at gene level so  for instance  it might not be the precise position  within a gene that's of  a particular interest i just want to  know which genes have you know  non-synonymous snips occurring in them  or  you know non-synonymous snips that  introduce frame shifts or  or  make  nonsense  and so i can create a filter and get  gene sets now an array star and then  start doing analysis on genes  so i have a couple different ways i can  filter gene level or snip level  and usually what we do with the ray star  is apply filters and create interesting  sets and that's kind of what i've done  here is i've applied a  snip level filter  and i picked uh one experiment here of  my three and i just said show me all the  non-synonymous snip so here's my  filtering that i did  and i also said give me a p-value of at  least 90 and the depth of 25.  all right i said find all the snips in  the phos sample that meet these criteria  and down here in the corner of the  window  there's 150 snips that meet that  criteria and here's a table now that  subset of snips i can see things like bb  snip rs numbers and group scores so so i  can save that information by clicking a  little icon here so that's and save the  results and use all the you know  interesting snips for this sample and i  can do the same thing for all the  samples  um and and create sets of snips with the  same filtering criteria and then i can  start to compare it might be control  versus cancer sets so it's a really nice  interface for creating these gene or  snip sets 